# Processing

This stage focuses on preparing high-quality reference audio for conditioning-based
text-to-speech inference.

No model training or fine-tuning is performed yet.
Instead, improvements are achieved through data-centric processing strategies.

---

## Overview

Processing consists of two major components:

1. Adaptive Speaker Clip Selection (ASCS)
2. Automatic style-oriented clip bucketing

These steps aim to improve speaker identity consistency during conditioning.

---

## Adaptive Speaker Clip Selection (ASCS)

XTTS speaker conditioning is highly sensitive to the quality of reference audio.
Low-energy or noisy clips can significantly degrade the generated voice.

To address this, a lightweight heuristic-based selection algorithm was applied.

### Features used

For each audio segment:

- Duration (seconds)
- RMS energy (average loudness)
- Silence ratio (percentage of near-zero amplitude)

### Selection strategy

Each clip is assigned a quality score based on:

- Higher duration (up to target segment length)
- Higher RMS energy
- Lower silence ratio

Clips are ranked and only the top-performing subset is retained.

This improves:

- embedding stability
- speaker identity clarity
- conditioning consistency

ASCS does not require training and operates fully offline.

---

## Automatic Clip Bucketing

Speaker reference audio can contain variations in delivery style such as:

- neutral narration
- expressive speech
- energetic delivery
- slower pacing

To explore how these variations influence conditioning output, clips are grouped into
style-oriented buckets using signal-level features.

### Features used

- RMS energy
- Pitch variance
- Silence ratio

Based on relative thresholds, clips are automatically grouped into:

- Neutral
- Expressive
- Energetic
- Slow

This bucketing is heuristic-based and not intended to be perfect.
It serves as a starting point for reference composition experiments.

---

## Outcome

Processing results in:

- a filtered set of high-quality speaker reference clips
- multiple style-based reference groups
- structured inputs for controlled inference experiments

These steps enable systematic evaluation without modifying model parameters.
